// Faithful English paraphrases of Ratner, George & Iveson (2012).
// Each lesson is bounded to its corresponding numbered chapter.
window.SFBT_CHAPTERS = [
{n:1,bookTitle:'What is Solution Focused Brief Therapy?',bookPage:3,steps:[
['The two sources of change','SFBT helps people build change quickly by describing a **preferred future** and by detailing skills, resources and successful occasions already present in their lives.'],
['Conversation is the intervention','The approach treats language as consequential: how clients describe life can open or close possibilities. The therapeutic interview itself, rather than expert advice after it, is the principal intervention.'],
['A deliberately radical focus','Clients may expect to describe problems, yet the practitioner does not cultivate extended problem talk or require a causal formulation. Attention is redirected toward solution talk while the client is still heard respectfully.'],
['Minimal footprints','Advice and prescribed homework are largely absent. **Insoo Kim Berg** urged practitioners to leave as few footprints as possible; any task is usually no more than noticing useful changes.'],
['What SFBT is not','It is not the therapist diagnosing the problem and supplying its solution. The desired direction, evidence of progress and workable resources must come from the client.'],
['Brief as a method','SFBT belongs to a brief-therapy tradition defined by a distinctive way of approaching human problems, not merely by shortening another therapy or imposing a session limit.']],quiz:[
['According to Key Point 1, what are the two principal sources of change in SFBT?',['A preferred-future description and detailed existing skills/resources','A diagnosis and a prescribed treatment plan','Insight into childhood causes and emotional catharsis','A ten-session limit and therapist homework'],0],
['What does the chapter identify as the main intervention?',['The therapeutic interview itself','Advice delivered after the interview','A standardized homework programme','A formal problem formulation'],0],
['What did Berg mean by leaving “few footprints”?',['Intervene as little and briefly as possible rather than directing the client’s life','Avoid keeping any clinical record','Never acknowledge the presenting difficulty','End every session within a fixed number of minutes'],0],
['Why is SFBT “brief” in the distinctive sense used here?',['Its philosophy and method are designed for economical solution development','Every client is discharged after exactly three sessions','It compresses psychodynamic formulation into less time','It avoids all conversation about client experience'],0]],audit:'PASS — definition, boundaries, intervention and brief-therapy distinction checked against book pp. 3–5.'},
{n:2,bookTitle:'The origins of Solution Focused Brief Therapy (1): Milton Erickson',bookPage:6,steps:[
['Who Erickson was','**Milton Erickson** was a psychiatrist and hypnotherapist whose practice influenced de Shazer, Ericksonian therapy, NLP and family therapy, despite Erickson claiming no formal theory.'],
['Utilize what arrives','Erickson called for full respect for and utilization of whatever the client presents. The work begins with the person’s own material rather than demanding that the person fit a prescribed model.'],
['Present and future adjustment','He emphasized what a patient does now and will do next, giving less weight to insight into remote causes. This orientation became important to brief therapy.'],
['The crystal ball precursor','In the **crystal ball technique**, clients imagined a future in which the difficulty had been successfully overcome. De Shazer saw this as a foundation for solution-based therapy and a precursor of the Miracle Question.'],
['Expect change','Erickson approached clients as if change were possible and inevitable. De Shazer linked this to the Buddhist idea that change is continual while stability is an illusion.'],
['The full inheritance','The chapter lists utilization, non-normative practice, limited interest in past insight, the crystal ball, tasks, and therapist responsibility for outcome as Erickson’s key contributions.']],quiz:[
['Which Ericksonian technique is presented as a precursor to the Miracle Question?',['The crystal ball technique','Reframing the attempted solution','A 0–10 progress scale','The First Session Formula Task'],0],
['What did Erickson’s principle of utilization require?',['Respect and make use of what the client presents','Replace the client’s account with a diagnostic formulation','Apply one standard task to every client','Concentrate on unconscious childhood conflict'],0],
['Which time orientation did Erickson emphasize in the passage cited by de Shazer?',['Present and future adjustment','Remote history and causal insight','Past diagnosis and prognosis','Only the moment before the session'],0],
['Which item is NOT part of the chapter’s summary of Erickson’s influence?',['A mandatory ten-session contract','Non-normative practice','Setting tasks','Therapist responsibility for success or failure'],0]],audit:'PASS — Erickson biography, utilization, crystal ball and six-item summary checked against book pp. 6–7.'},
{n:3,bookTitle:'Origins (2): family therapy and the Brief Therapy Center at the Mental Research Institute in Palo Alto',bookPage:8,steps:[
['The Palo Alto centre','The **Mental Research Institute** was founded by Don Jackson in 1959; its Brief Therapy Center began in 1967 and helped generate strategic family therapy.'],
['Interactional patterns','John Weakland, Paul Watzlawick and Dick Fisch examined communication patterns and homeostasis around the identified client rather than locating pathology solely inside one person.'],
['Attempted solutions','A normal difficulty can become a continuing problem through repeated, logical or customary attempts to solve it. Therapy therefore interrupts, reverses or otherwise changes the attempted solution.'],
['Here-and-now influence','The MRI team accepted the complaint at face value rather than searching for underlying causes. Influenced by Erickson, they used language to frame behavioural tasks that could alter present interaction.'],
['Paradox and reframing','They sometimes advised clients to “go slow,” producing a paradoxical push toward change, and used **reframing** to give behaviour a surprising new meaning. The salesman’s stammer was reframed as attention-catching.'],
['Ten sessions in the bank','MRI offered a maximum of ten sessions. Clients improving earlier could keep unused sessions “in the bank,” illustrating a planned brief format tied to reported outcomes.']],quiz:[
['What did the MRI team mean by an “attempted solution”?',['A repeated response that may maintain or worsen the difficulty','A preferred-future description already happening','A client’s numerical rating of progress','A compliment offered during the thinking pause'],0],
['What was the purpose of telling some clients to “go slow”?',['To use paradox so that clients might move toward change','To establish a compulsory waiting period','To gather a complete developmental history','To prevent clients from using remaining sessions'],0],
['How was the salesman’s stammer used in the chapter’s example?',['It was reframed as a way of capturing customers’ attention','It was diagnosed before any intervention','It was rated on a Miracle Scale','It was treated by rehearsing fluent speech'],0],
['What session arrangement belonged to the MRI Brief Therapy Center?',['A maximum of ten, with unused sessions kept in reserve','An average of three with no stated maximum','Exactly one session for every complaint','Open-ended meetings until causal insight emerged'],0]],audit:'PASS — MRI history, attempted solutions, paradox, reframing example and session limit checked against book pp. 8–9.'},
{n:4,bookTitle:'Origins (3): the Brief Family Therapy Center in Milwaukee and the birth of a new approach',bookPage:10,steps:[
['From Palo Alto to Milwaukee','John Weakland introduced **Steve de Shazer** to **Insoo Kim Berg**. They later established the Brief Family Therapy Center in Milwaukee with a diverse creative team.'],
['Early continuity and innovation','The early work resembled MRI practice: identify behavioural patterns, seek minimal goals and devise tasks. It also adopted crystal-ball imagery and other-person-perspective questions.'],
['Cooperation, not resistance','Erickson’s utilization inspired ways of cooperating with clients. De Shazer reframed “resistance” as the client’s unique attempt to cooperate, developed in “The Death of Resistance.”'],
['The First Session Formula Task','Faced with a family listing 23 problems, the team asked them to notice what happened that they wanted to continue. They returned noticing useful events and no longer needed further therapy.'],
['A generic task changes the model','Research in 1984 gave the **FSFT** to every client. Its success challenged the rule that every task must be tailored to the presenting problem and contributed directly to solution-focused practice.'],
['Exceptions and skeleton keys','The task made small unnoticed differences visible as exceptions. It was considered a “skeleton key”: one broadly useful key could open many different problem locks.']],quiz:[
['What event led to the First Session Formula Task in this chapter?',['A family presented a list of 23 problems and the team asked what they wanted to continue','A salesman asked how to reduce his stammer','A client rated progress at zero','A school class began the WOWW project'],0],
['What did the 1984 FSFT study challenge?',['The rule that each therapeutic task must fit a specific problem presentation','The view that language has contextual meaning','The use of a maximum of ten MRI sessions','The idea that clients can evaluate outcomes'],0],
['How did de Shazer reinterpret client resistance?',['As a unique way of attempting to cooperate','As proof that the contract was unrealistic','As a reason to intensify interpretation','As evidence of an underlying disorder'],0],
['Why was the FSFT described as a “skeleton key”?',['The same general task could unlock change across many different complaints','It supplied a separate key for each diagnosis','It reconstructed a complete family history','It guaranteed that therapy ended after one session'],0]],audit:'PASS — Milwaukee origins, cooperation, 23-problem family, FSFT research and skeleton-key meaning checked against book pp. 10–11.'},
{n:5,bookTitle:'The Brief Family Therapy Center: the first phase',bookPage:12,steps:[
['The interview becomes therapeutic','Early work relied on an observing team to construct tasks, but de Shazer increasingly saw the interview itself as therapeutic; by 1985 he called a team stimulating rather than necessary.'],
['Scales emerge from clients','Clients in the early 1970s were credited with teaching de Shazer the value of 0/1–10 scales. Scales helped people make vague experience more definable through a number.'],
['Ockham’s Razor','De Shazer drew on **William of Ockham**: what can be done with fewer means is done in vain with many. The model sought the minimum necessary for effective therapy.'],
['Exceptions define the phase','Between 1982 and 1987, the model concentrated on finding exceptions and expanding them. The 1986 paper announced “focused solution development” in deliberate contrast with MRI problem resolution.'],
['Change before therapy','The pre-therapy-change project found roughly two-thirds reported improvement when asked to look out for changes before the first appointment. The therapist assists and amplifies change rather than starting it.'],
['Why Miller called it a first phase','Gale Miller labelled this period “ecosystemic brief therapy,” arguing it still emphasized pathological communication patterns and disrupting them; the Miracle Question would mark a more fully solution-focused turn.']],quiz:[
['What did the pre-therapy-change project reportedly find?',['About two-thirds noticed improvement before the first appointment','No client changed before receiving a task','Scales worked only for precisely defined problems','Observing teams were required for improvement'],0],
['How did scales initially help vague clients?',['They allowed experience and progress to be defined by a number','They identified the correct diagnosis','They prescribed how many sessions were needed','They measured therapist competence'],0],
['What principle did Ockham’s Razor add to the emerging model?',['Use the minimum means needed for effective work','Every problem needs a unique complex task','A full observing team is always essential','Past causes must precede future questions'],0],
['Why did Gale Miller hesitate to call this phase fully solution focused?',['It still sought problematic communication patterns and tasks to disrupt them','It had already abandoned tasks entirely','It centred exclusively on preferred-future imagery','It rejected exceptions and pre-session change'],0]],audit:'PASS — observing team shift, scales, Ockham, exceptions, pre-therapy research and Miller’s critique checked against book pp. 12–13.'},
{n:6,bookTitle:'The Brief Family Therapy Center: the second phase',bookPage:14,steps:[
['The Miracle Question arrives','**Insoo Kim Berg** first used the Miracle Question in the early 1980s. Its importance grew slowly until de Shazer presented it as a cornerstone in *Clues* (1988).'],
['More than goal setting','At first it seemed another way to define goals. The team then noticed answers were richer: clients imaginatively pictured the post-miracle day while remaining concrete and realistic.'],
['An experienced description','Clients sometimes moved and behaved as though experiencing what they described. The question therefore did more than gather information; the conversation could create an embodied experience of difference.'],
['The revised first session','After briefly hearing what brought the client, the therapist invited a post-miracle description, then asked for recent exceptions or “pieces of the miracle,” followed by a progress scale.'],
['The Miracle Scale','The scale located the client in relation to the described goals. Because it followed the preferred-future picture, it became known as the **Miracle Scale**.'],
['Tasks recede','The centre moved far from collecting data for an observing team’s tailored homework. Tasks were reduced mainly to noticing signs of the miracle or occasionally pretending it had begun.']],quiz:[
['Who first used the Miracle Question according to this chapter?',['Insoo Kim Berg','Milton Erickson','John Weakland','Gale Miller'],0],
['What made Miracle Question responses different from ordinary goal statements?',['They often produced rich, concrete and almost experienced descriptions','They revealed the hidden cause of the complaint','They determined the diagnosis and treatment dose','They required hypnosis before clients could answer'],0],
['What sequence characterized the revised first-session procedure?',['Post-miracle description, pieces already happening, then a progress scale','Diagnosis, causal formulation, advice, then homework','Scale first, problem history, then interpretation','Reframing, paradoxical instruction, then ten-session contract'],0],
['What happened to homework tasks in this second phase?',['They largely shrank to noticing or enacting signs of the miracle','They became more individually elaborate','They replaced the therapeutic interview','They were mandatory after every question'],0]],audit:'PASS — authorship, development, experiential quality, first-session sequence and reduced tasks checked against book pp. 14–15.'},
{n:7,bookTitle:'Solution Focused Brief Therapy today',bookPage:16,steps:[
['After the Milwaukee flowering','Following the creative 1982–1994 period, BFTC effectively disbanded. De Shazer increasingly pursued philosophy, while Berg extended application into diverse services.'],
['Berg takes practice outward','Berg wrote for families, problem drinking, child protection, substance misuse, children, coaching and schools, and brought the approach beyond therapy rooms into streets and soup kitchens.'],
['Solution-focused practice','When used by coaches, mentors, nurses or social workers, the work is often called **solution-focused practice**. Multiple contemporary versions reflect different degrees of continuity and development.'],
['BRIEF’s continuation','BRIEF sees its work as continuing BFTC’s tradition through Ockham’s Razor and checking outcome consistency from the client’s viewpoint. Development is allowed without abandoning the underlying philosophy.'],
['Two BRIEF adjustments','BRIEF reduced the centrality of the Miracle Question and rarely gave homework. De Shazer knew the reasons and welcomed these attempts to carry the brief-therapy tradition forward.'],
['Growing acceptance','The chapter charts expansion through training, UK audit recognition in 2010, professional associations and international uptake across North America, Europe, Australasia and Asia.']],quiz:[
['Why is “solution-focused practice” often used instead of SFBT?',['The approach is used within non-therapy roles such as coaching, nursing and social work','The Miracle Question has been legally renamed','Only BFTC members may use the term SFBT','It refers exclusively to self-help books'],0],
['Which two adjustments does the chapter associate with BRIEF?',['Less central Miracle Question use and very rare homework','More diagnosis and longer homework programmes','A fixed ten-session maximum and mandatory teams','Removal of client outcome feedback and scales'],0],
['What principle does BRIEF explicitly retain from de Shazer?',['Ockham’s Razor—the minimum needed in a session','Every task must fit a specific diagnosis','All progress must be independently rated','Resistance must be confronted directly'],0],
['What is the chapter’s main picture of SFBT today?',['An internationally applied family of related practices with a continuing core tradition','A historical method no longer used after BFTC','A model restricted to private psychotherapy rooms','One unchanging script controlled by a single association'],0]],audit:'PASS — post-BFTC development, Berg applications, terminology, BRIEF adjustments and international acceptance checked against book pp. 16–17.'},
{n:8,bookTitle:'Philosophical underpinnings: constructivism',bookPage:18,steps:[
['A different person after description','De Shazer’s statement that a client is different after the Miracle Question is meant literally within **constructivism**: reality is invented or constructed rather than simply discovered.'],
['The shift from objectivism','Constructivism moves away from the belief that therapy discovers a single objective reality. A new description can therefore participate in creating a different lived reality.'],
['Diagnosis as an example','The chapter uses “depression” to illustrate concern about objectification. Treating a diagnostic description as a fixed thing can make it appear as immutable as other facts about identity.'],
['Problems remain painful','Constructed does not mean imaginary or trivial. Difficult and painful realities are acknowledged, while their meaning and status arise through how people negotiate and describe experience.'],
['Talking into and out of trouble','Gale Miller’s formulation is that people may talk themselves into problems and therapy can be a process of talking them out—changing descriptions changes available possibilities.'],
['Clinical implication','The practitioner need not argue that the client’s present account is false. Instead, conversation makes room for alternative descriptions that are more useful for movement and agency.']],quiz:[
['In this chapter, what does constructivism claim about reality?',['It is constructed or invented rather than simply discovered as one fixed object','It is entirely imaginary and pain is unreal','It can only be established through diagnosis','It remains unchanged by language or relationship'],0],
['Why does the chapter discuss “depression”?',['To show how a description can become objectified as a fixed reality','To recommend a particular diagnostic interview','To deny that depressed people experience pain','To identify the cause of every presenting problem'],0],
['What does the chapter mean when it says realities are constructed?',['Their meaning emerges through negotiated descriptions, even when suffering is real','Clients can choose never to experience difficulty','Therapists should dispute every client statement','Only positive words may be used in therapy'],0],
['Why might the Miracle Question leave a client “a different person”?',['Participating in a new description creates new possibilities and experience','It provides a definitive medical diagnosis','It erases every past event from memory','It imposes the therapist’s preferred identity'],0]],audit:'PASS — constructivism, objectivism, diagnosis example, negotiated reality and language implication checked against book p. 18.'},
{n:9,bookTitle:'Philosophical underpinnings: Wittgenstein, language, and social constructionism',bookPage:19,steps:[
['Language games','Following **Ludwig Wittgenstein**, words gain meaning through context and rules of use. Problem talk and solution talk are different language games, not neutral containers for the same reality.'],
['Problem talk and solution talk','Problem talk commonly points backward and implies permanence; solution talk is more hopeful and future directed, implying transience and constructing the solution jointly in conversation.'],
['Emotion and outward criteria','The linguistic focus does not ban emotion. De Shazer treated emotions as part of language and followed Wittgenstein’s view that an inner process needs outward criteria, hence attention to observable behaviour.'],
['From constructivism to social construction','Constructivism locates world-making in the individual mind; **social constructionism** locates what becomes real in relationships and socially supplied categories.'],
['A cooperative relationship','Because realities are relationally constructed, SFBT asks about other people’s perspectives and builds cooperation. The therapist is expert in useful questions, not expert on the client’s life.'],
['Description rather than explanation','De Shazer resisted theories claiming to explain how therapy works. Philosophy should illuminate description; practitioners describe what they see and hear and avoid interpretive assertions.']],quiz:[
['What is a Wittgensteinian “language game” in this chapter?',['A context in which words gain meaning through shared rules of use','A scripted vocabulary test for clients','A technique for avoiding emotional words','A theory that words have one fixed meaning'],0],
['How does social constructionism differ from constructivism here?',['It emphasizes realities emerging through social relationships rather than only inside an individual mind','It restores one objective diagnostic reality','It excludes other-person perspectives','It makes the therapist the expert on the client’s future'],0],
['How does the chapter respond to the claim that linguistic SFBT ignores emotion?',['Emotion can be talked about, with attention also given to outward criteria and behaviour','Emotion is declared irrelevant to every client','Only bodily emotion is accepted as real','The therapist interprets hidden emotion without asking'],0],
['What expertise does the therapist retain in the cooperative relationship?',['Expertise in asking useful questions, not expertise on the client’s life','Expertise in deciding when the client is cured','Expertise in supplying political or personal values','Expertise in interpreting unseen inner structures'],0]],audit:'PASS — language games, talk distinction, emotion, social construction, expertise and descriptive stance checked against book pp. 19–20.'},
{n:10,bookTitle:'Assumptions in Solution Focused Brief Therapy',bookPage:21,steps:[
['Motivation and cooperation','All clients are motivated toward something. The practitioner discovers that direction and cooperates with the client’s unique attempt to cooperate rather than explaining behaviour as resistance.'],
['What is not required','Understanding the cause is not necessary for resolution and extended problem discussion can sometimes be unhelpful. Successful work depends instead on knowing what the client wants.'],
['Exceptions and economy','However fixed a pattern appears, there are occasions when the client does part of the solution. The economical response is to help the client identify and do more of what already works.'],
['No underlying pathology assumption','Problems are treated as things clients want to do without, not evidence of hidden pathology. In most cases clients are best placed to judge when resolution is sufficient.'],
['Small change and who attends','A small difference can initiate larger change. It is not always necessary to meet everyone involved—or even the person identified as having the problem—for useful change to occur.'],
['Rules and major tenets','De Shazer’s rules are: do not fix what is not broken; do more of what works; if it does not work, do something different. Added tenets include small steps, exceptions, distinct solution language and a negotiable future.']],quiz:[
['How does this chapter reinterpret apparent lack of motivation?',['Clients are motivated toward something, which the practitioner must discover','Unmotivated clients cannot benefit from SFBT','Resistance must be overcome through confrontation','Motivation is supplied by prescribed homework'],0],
['Which statement matches de Shazer’s three rules?',['If something works, do more; if not, do something different','Always repeat an intervention until the client complies','Fix every area even when the client reports no problem','Understand the cause before attempting any change'],0],
['What assumption supports asking about exceptions?',['No problem happens all the time; clients already do parts of solutions','Exceptions are diagnostic symptoms','Only large changes can alter a stable pattern','Past success matters only after causal insight'],0],
['Who is usually the best judge that a problem is resolved?',['The client','The observing team','The diagnostic manual','Every family member unanimously'],0]],audit:'PASS — seven assumptions, three rules and five additional tenets checked against book pp. 21–22.'},
{n:11,bookTitle:'The client–therapist relationship',bookPage:23,steps:[
['From three relationship types','In 1988 de Shazer described **visitor**, **complainant** and **customer** relationships. Visitors had no complaint of their own; complainants recognized difficulty but did not propose their own action; customers wanted to act.'],
['The classification is abandoned','By 1991 de Shazer said there was no reluctant client: everyone is a customer for something, perhaps simply getting another person “off their back.” Assessing motivation distracts from discovering that something.'],
['Jessica’s good reason','A school student said she had been sent, yet careful questions revealed a reason to cooperate: avoiding exclusion and staying at school in a way acceptable to her and the school. The assumption of motivation shaped useful questions.'],
['Client purpose governs','The practitioner acknowledges difficulty but has no therapeutic goal beyond the client’s formulation. In statutory work, a viable client hope is negotiated within legal and institutional requirements.'],
['No wrong answers','Whatever clients do is treated as their best attempt to help the process. Reading an answer as resistance signals that the practitioner should listen more closely and try something different.'],
['Expertise in questions','The therapist knows ways of talking that support discovery, often building the next question from the client’s last words. The client remains expert on life, values, culture, usefulness and completion.']],quiz:[
['What change in de Shazer’s thinking occurred by 1991?',['He rejected the idea of reluctant clients and assumed everyone wanted something from the meeting','He made visitor, complainant and customer permanent diagnoses','He advised assigning tasks to every visitor','He stopped asking what clients wanted'],0],
['What useful purpose emerged in the conversation with Jessica?',['Finding a way to remain at school','Proving that she enjoyed counselling','Diagnosing the cause of her behaviour','Persuading her to accept exclusion'],0],
['In this chapter, what should the practitioner infer from apparent “resistance”?',['The practitioner may not be listening closely enough and should change approach','The client has answered incorrectly','The client lacks all motivation','A stronger prescription is required'],0],
['Where does therapist expertise lie?',['In building useful questions from client answers','In knowing the client’s correct life choices','In judging outcome without client feedback','In uncovering hidden meanings behind every complaint'],0]],audit:'PASS — classification history, 1991 revision, Jessica example and six relationship assumptions checked against book pp. 23–26.'},
{n:12,bookTitle:'The evidence that Solution Focused Brief Therapy works',bookPage:27,steps:[
['The evidence base in 2011','Macdonald identified 97 relevant studies, two meta-analyses and 17 randomized controlled trials showing benefit; nine showed benefit beyond existing methods. Twenty-six of 34 comparisons favoured solution focus.'],
['Effectiveness and brevity','Data from more than 4,000 cases suggested success above 60 per cent within three to five sessions. The chapter presents this as encouraging effectiveness evidence, not a guarantee for an individual.'],
['Breadth of application','Studies covered offending, substance misuse, domestic violence, couples and parents, older people, physical difficulties, mental health, children and education. No clear demographic or presenting-problem exclusions had emerged.'],
['Comparable, not universally superior','The authors conclude that evidence suggests broad effectiveness and good comparability with other treatments. Ethical practitioners may attempt SFBT widely while continuing to monitor response.'],
['When to change course','Beyebach’s Salamanca findings support “if it doesn’t work, do something different”: no improvement after three sessions indicates considering a different model or therapist.'],
['Durability challenge','Isebaert’s alcohol-use study reported about half abstinent at four years and a further quarter drinking in a controlled way, challenging the claim that brief work produces only short-lived change.']],quiz:[
['What did the effectiveness data for more than 4,000 cases suggest?',['Over 60 per cent success within three to five sessions','Universal recovery after one session','Benefit only for school problems','No difference from a waiting list in every study'],0],
['What action does the Salamanca research support after three sessions without improvement?',['Consider changing the model or therapist','Continue unchanged for ten sessions','End all support immediately','Assume the client is resistant'],0],
['What conclusion does the chapter draw about exclusion criteria?',['Studies had not established clear demographic or problem-based exclusions','SFBT should exclude chronic problems','Only adults qualify for treatment','Substance misuse is contraindicated'],0],
['Why is Isebaert’s four-year finding important here?',['It challenges the idea that brief therapy can produce only short-term effects','It proves every client must abstain','It establishes a mandatory inpatient model','It replaces all randomized trials'],0]],audit:'PASS — study counts, effectiveness, application range, change criterion and long-term alcohol findings checked against book pp. 27–28.'},
{n:13,bookTitle:'How brief is “brief”?',bookPage:29,steps:[
['Not simply time limited','SFBT has been packaged as fixed-term, group and single-session work, yet traditionally “brief” does not mean a predetermined short programme.'],
['Client-determined brevity','De Shazer’s definition is “as long as it takes and not one session more.” Clients decide when enough has been achieved and whether another meeting is useful.'],
['The apparent paradox','Giving clients control does not lengthen typical work: cited averages were three to five sessions, and BRIEF clients averaged fewer than four. Brevity emerges from assumptions and practice.'],
['Start work immediately','There is no separate initial assessment phase. Change may already be happening before the first meeting, and clients bring solution patterns as well as problem patterns.'],
['Practices that save time','Work stays close to the client’s best hopes, treats each session as potentially last, avoids battles over resistance and builds on actions already working.'],
['Ripples and spacing','A small systemic change can ripple through relationships. Longer gaps between sessions give clients time to live differently; four sessions may therefore extend over ten weeks or more.']],quiz:[
['What is de Shazer’s definition of brief therapy in this chapter?',['As long as it takes and not one session more','No more than ten sessions','Exactly three sessions for every client','One lengthy session only'],0],
['Who ultimately determines how brief the work is?',['The client, by deciding when enough has been achieved','The referring agency alone','A standardized scale cut-off','The therapist before the first meeting'],0],
['Which assumption contributes to time efficiency?',['Change may already be happening before the first meeting','A full assessment must precede any therapeutic work','Every involved person must attend','Between-session intervals should be as short as possible'],0],
['Why may four sessions extend across ten weeks?',['Longer intervals give clients time to do something different','The therapist needs weeks to formulate a diagnosis','A ten-week delay is mandatory','Sessions cannot occur during reported improvement'],0]],audit:'PASS — client-determined definition and nine time-efficiency assumptions checked against book pp. 29–30.'},
{n:14,bookTitle:'Summary: the structure of solution focused sessions',bookPage:31,steps:[
['Three organizing questions','A first session asks: what are the client’s **best hopes**; what would be different if achieved; and what is already in place that could contribute to them becoming real.'],
['Direction comes first','Every therapist begins by establishing the hoped-for outcome, because without an agreed destination the conversation cannot be steered responsibly. Future description and existing foundation may follow in either order.'],
['Problem-free opening and contract','An optional problem-free conversation meets the person. Contracting is essential: BRIEF asks what the client’s best hopes are from the work.'],
['Describe and locate the future','The Tomorrow Question develops everyday signs of hopes achieved. The therapist then searches for current or past instances and may use a scale with 10 representing the preferred future.'],
['Close by highlighting utility','A thinking break may precede a summary that acknowledges struggle and appreciates hopes and successes. The close foregrounds material associated with further progress.'],
['Follow-up starts with change','Later sessions usually begin “What’s better?” rather than repeating contracting and future work. Progress is amplified and scaled; when absent or worse, coping and exception questions remain available.']],quiz:[
['Which question must come first in the structure described?',['What are your best hopes from our meeting(s)?','What number are you today?','When did the problem begin?','Who else should attend?'],0],
['What does 10 represent on the progress scale in this chapter?',['The preferred future achieved','The worst the problem has ever been','Ten required sessions','Maximum client effort'],0],
['How do follow-up sessions usually open?',['What’s better since we last met?','Please repeat your full problem history','Have you completed the assigned treatment?','What diagnosis now fits best?'],0],
['Are the listed session elements rigid rules?',['No, the authors present them as guidelines','Yes, every stage must use exact wording','Only the closing is optional','Yes, future description must always precede foundation'],0]],audit:'PASS — three core questions, five first-session elements and follow-up structure checked against book pp. 31–34.'},
{n:15,bookTitle:'Ideas about therapeutic conversation',bookPage:37,steps:[
['Purposeful conversation','Therapy is conversation directed toward a purpose, and in SFBT the client defines that purpose. The practitioner directs the conversation toward the client’s stated end.'],
['Turn-taking','The first core idea is that participants take turns. Equal opportunity to have a voice allows each person to participate in shaping the conversational world.'],
['Why interruption matters','Turn-taking becomes visible when it is broken. Repeatedly losing one’s turn marginalizes and diminishes a speaker—an experience familiar to many clients and disenfranchised groups.'],
['Disciplined space for voice','Within the therapy room, a practitioner can offer equality of voice by having enough discipline to speak in turn rather than taking over the conversation.'],
['Build on the previous turn','The second core idea is that each turn follows and builds on the last. This continuity makes creativity and effective therapeutic co-construction possible.'],
['Influence in the client’s favour','Conversation continually co-creates a view of the world. The therapist influences that evolving view in the client’s favour through careful choices of questions.']],quiz:[
['Who defines the purpose of therapeutic conversation in SFBT?',['The client','The diagnostic team','The referring agency in every case','The therapist’s preferred model'],0],
['What are the chapter’s two simple ideas about conversation?',['Turn-taking, and each turn building on the previous one','Interpretation, followed by advice','Assessment, followed by treatment','Silence, followed by homework'],0],
['Why is turn-taking ethically significant in this chapter?',['Losing one’s voice can marginalize and diminish a person','It ensures every session has equal duration','It prevents any expression of disagreement','It allows the therapist to speak first each time'],0],
['How does the therapist influence a co-created view?',['By carefully choosing questions that build on the client’s turn','By interrupting incorrect descriptions','By supplying a finished worldview','By avoiding any conversational direction'],0]],audit:'PASS — purpose, turn-taking, marginalization, building turns and influence checked against book p. 37.'},
{n:16,bookTitle:'Choosing the next question',bookPage:38,steps:[
['Professional choice is deliberate','Unlike casual conversation, each professional contribution should be selected for likely usefulness to the client or necessity for the task. The next question builds creatively on the current answer.'],
['Models select different threads','A multifaceted answer offers many possible follow-ups. Historical, systemic and cognitive models may select causes, relationships or thoughts; the model shapes what the practitioner hears.'],
['Follow the answer to best hopes','When a client mentions depression, her mother, her husband and wanting to feel better, SFBT follows the element answering the best-hopes question: the hope to feel better.'],
['Tone acknowledges difficulty','Following the hope does not erase hardship. The content affects tone, while the question asks for a first sign of beginning to feel better rather than tracing a cause.'],
['Broadening','“What else?” invites additional elements of a description—for example another activity or relationship that would indicate improvement. It is common, simple and useful.'],
['Detailing','A detailing question stays with one element—where, who, what difference—before broadening again. Skilled interviewing alternates these two choices responsively.']],quiz:[
['In the chapter’s depression example, which part would an SFBT practitioner follow?',['The client’s hope to feel better','The mother’s history as the presumed cause','The husband’s evenings as the primary diagnosis','The phrase “give up” as a cognitive distortion'],0],
['What is the function of “What else?” here?',['To broaden the client’s description','To challenge the truth of the answer','To close the conversation','To establish a causal sequence'],0],
['Which question is an example of detailing?',['Where might you go when you begin getting out more?','What else would be different?','Are there any other hopes?','What other areas might improve?'],0],
['What should govern the next professional question?',['Its likely usefulness and how it builds on the client’s answer','A fixed list used regardless of the answer','The therapist’s curiosity about remote causes','The need to cover every element at once'],0]],audit:'PASS — model-shaped selection, depression example, tone, broadening and detailing checked against book pp. 38–39.'},
{n:17,bookTitle:'Acknowledgement and possibility',bookPage:40,steps:[
['Future focus is not problem phobia','SFBT must be where the client is as well as explore where life might go. The therapist listens carefully and acknowledges hardship before selecting a possibility-bearing element.'],
['The response shapes direction','“That was difficult—when did it begin?” develops history. “That was difficult—how did you handle it?” acknowledges the same pain while opening evidence of capability.'],
['Hardship implies survival','Paradoxically, severe or prolonged difficulty makes continuing to live with it an achievement. Perseverance and determination can become foundations for future possibility.'],
['Miss Brown’s thread of hope','With Abel near school exclusion, the therapist acknowledged the teacher’s distress and competence, then asked what she saw that made therapy worth trying. She remembered Abel singing “like a little angel.”'],
['Gloria’s fighting','Abel’s mother linked her illness to his trouble and feared exclusion. Her identity as a fighter was acknowledged, then connected to what worthwhile change would look like: Abel returning home happy.'],
['Possibility becomes observable','Teacher, mother and child developed concrete signs of a better school morning. Abel described and enacted sitting, lining up and walking quietly; the teacher’s changed view may already have helped change begin.']],quiz:[
['Which response best combines acknowledgement and possibility?',['That sounds extremely hard; how have you managed to keep going?','When did the difficulty first begin?','You should focus only on positive things now','The problem is less serious than it feels'],0],
['What small thread of hope did Miss Brown identify in Abel?',['When he sang, she saw a likeable boy behind the behaviour','He had completed all assigned homework','He wanted to leave the school','He never disrupted morning assembly'],0],
['What future sign did Gloria name?',['Abel would come home from school happy','Her illness would be cured overnight','The school would withdraw every rule','Abel would explain the cause of his behaviour'],0],
['What is the chapter’s central correction to a misconception?',['Solution focus requires acknowledgement of pain, not avoidance of it','Acknowledgement means staying with problem history only','Possibility should be introduced before listening','Greater hardship leaves fewer visible strengths'],0]],audit:'PASS — acknowledgment contrast and Abel/Miss Brown/Gloria case sequence checked against book pp. 40–42.'},
{n:18,bookTitle:'Compliments',bookPage:43,steps:[
['A disciplined closing practice','Affirmative feedback has been part of SFBT from the start, but compliments are not casual praise. Lazy or patronizing comments damage therapist credibility.'],
['Honest and evidenced','A useful compliment rests on a specific behaviour the client has described, so the therapist can explain its basis if asked.'],
['Relevant and earned','It connects to the client’s purpose and to something the client has achieved, preferably through effort. It must be offered in a form the client can accept and agree with.'],
['Not persuasion','The therapist does not use praise to impose a positive view. Instead of insisting a self-doubting person “has confidence,” ask how they managed to act confidently without feeling it.'],
['No strings attached','A compliment is unconditional, not pressure for behaviour the therapist wants. “You did well—keep it up” reveals the giver’s agenda and is less credible.'],
['Compliments inside questions','With experience, affirmation can be built into questions such as how a client produced good work amid difficulty. Retaining an end-of-session ritual trains beginners to notice strengths and achievements.']],quiz:[
['Which quality is required of a solution-focused compliment?',['It is evidence-based and relevant to the client’s purpose','It presents the therapist’s preferred positive interpretation','It pressures the client to repeat a desired behaviour','It praises personality without reference to action'],0],
['Why is “You did well—keep it up” problematic in this chapter?',['The added instruction makes the compliment conditional and agenda-driven','The statement contains too little optimism','Compliments may never mention effort','It should be given only before the session starts'],0],
['How should a therapist respond when behaviour looks confident but the client says confidence is absent?',['Ask how the client managed to act confidently despite not feeling it','Correct the client and insist confidence is present','Avoid mentioning the discrepancy','Diagnose hidden confidence'],0],
['Why retain an end-of-session compliment ritual while learning SFBT?',['It disciplines attention toward evidence of strengths and achievement throughout the session','It guarantees client agreement','It replaces the need to listen during the session','It provides leverage for assigning homework'],0]],audit:'PASS — honesty, evidence, relevance, acceptability, unconditionality and question-form compliments checked against book pp. 43–44.'},
{n:19,bookTitle:'Deciding who to meet with',bookPage:45,steps:[
['A systemic inheritance','From family therapy, SFBT retains the view that change in one element or relationship can affect other parts of a family, team, friendship group or organization.'],
['The ripple reaches absentees','De Shazer’s early research found improvement in issues and relationships not discussed directly. Change can travel beyond the person present in the room.'],
['Attendance did not predict outcome','BRIEF research found no obvious relationship between who attended and reported improvement. Even the person identified as the patient need not necessarily attend.'],
['No evidence for expert prescription','If attendance does not predict outcome, the therapist lacks a basis for prescribing who must come. The client knows practical circumstances better and is invited to decide.'],
['An informed flexible choice','The therapist explains that some come alone or with family and others protect work or school commitments. Start with the client’s judgement and do something different later if needed.'],
['No negative stories about absence','Whoever comes is treated as the right person or people. The therapist does not invent resistant or pathological explanations for those who are elsewhere.']],quiz:[
['What did BRIEF research suggest about attendance and outcome?',['No obvious correlation was found','The identified patient had to attend','Whole-family attendance doubled success','Individual attendance prevented ripple effects'],0],
['Who should usually decide who attends?',['The client, after receiving neutral information about options','The therapist, using a family diagnosis','The referrer in every circumstance','All absent family members unanimously'],0],
['What is the systemic rationale for flexible attendance?',['Change in one part of a system may affect other parts','Only direct participants can ever change','Absence proves resistance','Every relationship must be discussed explicitly'],0],
['How should the therapist understand a person’s absence?',['Avoid hypothesizing negative reasons; work with whoever attends','Treat it as refusal of treatment','Delay all work until everyone arrives','Assume the absent person is the cause'],0]],audit:'PASS — systemic ripple, de Shazer/BRIEF findings, client choice and non-pathologizing absence checked against book pp. 45–46.'},
{n:20,bookTitle:'Problem-free talk',bookPage:49,steps:[
['Three purposes','**Problem-free talk** meets the person rather than the problem, lets the therapist choose the client they will work with, and begins gathering resources useful for change.'],
['A few ordinary questions','For a few minutes the therapist asks about life outside the presenting difficulty: what the person does, enjoys, has learned or how the day is spent.'],
['Robert becomes visible','Nine-year-old Robert enthusiastically described building a powered model car in science. His curiosity, humour, articulation and cooperation became visible instead of only the exclusion-risk referral.'],
['Choosing the competent Robert','The therapist chose to work alongside the competent, collaborative boy revealed in conversation and kept that version of Robert present across four sessions.'],
['Resource gathering in detail','Even his broken model showed a resource: Robert did not mind because he knew how to build it again. The exchange supplied evidence of confidence, learning and persistence.'],
['Ordinary social wisdom','The practice adapts how people naturally meet socially—seeking common ground and what they value in another—rather than opening a first encounter by investigating problems.']],quiz:[
['What are the three purposes of problem-free talk?',['Meet the person, choose the client, and gather resources','Diagnose the problem, assign a task, and rate risk','Establish causation, test insight, and plan treatment','Invite family, review records, and set a session limit'],0],
['What did Robert discuss during the example?',['Building an electric model car in science','The incident leading to his referral','A progress scale about school','His preferred future after a miracle'],0],
['Why did Robert not mind that the model broke?',['He knew how to make it again','He disliked science','A teacher promised to replace it','The damage was part of his task'],0],
['What does “choosing the client” mean in this chapter?',['Relating to the resourceful person revealed in conversation rather than a problem-saturated referral','Selecting which referral to accept','Choosing who may attend the session','Deciding the client’s treatment goal'],0]],audit:'PASS — three purposes, ordinary questions and every material part of Robert’s example checked against book pp. 49–51.'},
{n:21,bookTitle:'Identifying resources',bookPage:52,steps:[
['An early training discovery','After BRIEF trainees added problem-free talk and resource identification to their existing work, many reported significant client changes within a week, including two apparent problem resolutions.'],
['Resources alter conversation','Attending to the resourceful side of clients helped them become more open. That changed the quality of conversation and appeared to accelerate change.'],
['Assets do not hide debts','Seeing strengths does not obscure problems, just as recording company assets does not conceal debts. Effective therapy ultimately enables clients to draw differently on their own resources.'],
['When the therapist loses hope','A **resource audit** is a self-supervision practice for moments when work feels repetitive or the therapist begins to dread a meeting. It restores sight of the person’s capabilities.'],
['Leidl’s audit','Before meeting Holocaust survivor Leidl, the therapist listed survival, perseverance, humour, generosity and hard work. The resourceful Leidl then re-entered his perception, and the work soon ended successfully.'],
['The practitioner’s responsibility','Failing to notice and name resources can narrow the therapist’s view and harm the work. Resource identification is therefore essential, not decorative positivity.']],quiz:[
['What change did BRIEF trainees make before reporting unexpectedly rapid improvement?',['They added problem-free talk and looked for client resources','They introduced diagnostic assessments','They doubled session frequency','They assigned identical homework'],0],
['What is a resource audit used for?',['Restoring the therapist’s view of client capabilities when hope is fading','Evaluating financial eligibility','Measuring progress from 0 to 10','Choosing family attendees'],0],
['Which resource was central to Leidl’s history?',['Surviving the Holocaust through her own resourcefulness','Completing a Miracle Question','Following therapist advice','Attending weekly groupwork'],0],
['What is the chapter’s accountant analogy?',['Ignoring strengths to see problems is like ignoring assets to see debts','Resources should be balanced numerically','Therapy requires a financial audit','Problems and assets are identical'],0]],audit:'PASS — training outcome, assets analogy, resource-audit rationale and Leidl case checked against book pp. 52–53.'},
{n:22,bookTitle:'Listening with a constructive ear: what the client can do, not what they cannot do',bookPage:54,steps:[
['A different listening direction','A **constructive ear** listens for building blocks of possibility rather than facts needed to explain, assess and treat what is wrong. Psychological theories are treated as metaphors, not objective truth.'],
['Acknowledgement remains','Constructive listening begins where the client is. It does not require a complete problem retelling, but it acknowledges difficulty and uses its severity to redefine the scale of achievement.'],
['A constructive question','“Given how down you feel and how hard transport is, how did you keep your word and attend?” indirectly recognizes the problem while directing curiosity toward capability.'],
['Gladys’s crisis','Recently discharged after an overdose, Gladys returned to a flooded home and a day of workmen. She ended the ordeal by chasing them to offer humorous thanks.'],
['Listening for humour','The therapist asked how Gladys retained humour during danger and distress. The question developed a story of friendliness, laughter and social connection rather than hospital readmission alone.'],
['A changed self-appraisal','By the end Gladys felt proud of weathering the crisis and recognized feeling more positive the next morning. The same event now evidenced coping rather than only vulnerability.']],quiz:[
['What does constructive listening seek?',['Building blocks for new possibilities in what the client can do','Evidence for one correct psychological theory','A complete causal account before intervention','Ways to contradict the client’s distress'],0],
['How does greater hardship affect the meaning of coping?',['It makes continuing and attending a greater achievement','It makes resources irrelevant','It requires problem talk only','It proves the client cannot change'],0],
['What resource did the therapist follow in Gladys’s story?',['Her sense of humour during a crisis','Her wish to return to hospital','Her compliance with a task','Her numerical rating'],0],
['What changed for Gladys by the end of the conversation?',['She felt proud of how she weathered the crisis','She discovered the pipe had never burst','She denied that the day was distressing','She received advice about home repair'],0]],audit:'PASS — theoretical contrast, constructive question and Gladys case checked against book pp. 54–55.'},
{n:23,bookTitle:'Constructive histories',bookPage:56,steps:[
['The story behind the story','Behind hardship lies struggle, behind setbacks perseverance and behind misfortune survival. The therapist hears the spoken history and asks toward the not-yet-spoken constructive history.'],
['Gerard’s long account','Seventy-year-old Gerard described 45 years of depression beginning after being blown up on active service. The therapist listened for nearly an hour and asked only eight questions.'],
['Questions reveal endurance','Questions asked how he kept going, coped with divorce, earned responsibility at work, overcame alcohol and drug dependence, and found confidence to form a relationship.'],
['Other-person evidence','Asking what an employer and future wife saw in him brought perspectives that contradicted a solely depressed identity without arguing against his account.'],
['A childhood ambition fulfilled','The final question assembled the whole history and asked how, despite every ordeal, Gerard fell in love and became a children’s train driver—his childhood ambition.'],
['A newly named life story','Gerard called it an unusual life story rather than only a history of depression. Weeks later the referrer reported he sought to stop medication and showed no withdrawal signs.']],quiz:[
['What is a “constructive history”?',['The not-yet-spoken story of struggle, perseverance and survival within hardship','A corrected chronological record','A denial of painful events','A diagnostic developmental history'],0],
['How many questions did the therapist ask during Gerard’s long account?',['Eight','Forty-five','Three','None'],0],
['What childhood ambition had Gerard eventually fulfilled?',['Becoming a train driver','Becoming a therapist','Joining the military','Writing a memoir'],0],
['What was distinctive about the therapist’s questions?',['Each invited evidence of how Gerard managed, overcame or was valued','Each sought the original cause of depression','Each challenged the accuracy of his memory','Each prescribed a next action'],0]],audit:'PASS — story-behind-story formulation, all eight Gerard questions and reported follow-up checked against book pp. 56–57.'},
{n:24,bookTitle:'Pre-meeting change',bookPage:58,steps:[
['Change before the first session','**Pre-meeting change** is improvement occurring after help is arranged but before the first meeting. Freud called it “flight into health”; SFBT treats it as natural spontaneous recovery.'],
['Commitment changes attention','Committing to the possibility of a solution may make a person more open—consciously or not—to different thoughts and actions before therapy formally begins.'],
['Research and invitation','Weiner-Davis reported positive pre-meeting change in about 70 per cent, associated with good prognosis. Practitioners may invite clients to notice changes before the appointment.'],
['George misses his success','Bullied by a business partner, George wanted confidence to stand up for himself. Yet the day before therapy he had refused to pick up files dropped at his feet and calmly required they be brought to his office.'],
['Problem attention conceals progress','George replayed the incident as evidence of fear and weakness, failing to notice he had already done what he sought. His partner had subsequently become friendly.'],
['The therapist takes the back seat','When pre-session change is noticed, the client is immediately visible as a person who solves problems. The therapist can amplify that achievement rather than claim to initiate it.']],quiz:[
['How does SFBT interpret pre-meeting improvement?',['As natural change to notice and amplify','As pathological flight from reality','As proof therapy is unnecessary in every case','As compliance with an assigned intervention'],0],
['What proportion in the cited research experienced positive pre-meeting change?',['About 70 per cent','About 10 per cent','Exactly half','All participants'],0],
['What had George already done before therapy?',['He stood up to his business partner over the dropped files','He left the business','He completed a confidence scale','He apologized for challenging his partner'],0],
['What stance becomes possible after noticing such change?',['The therapist can take a back seat to the client’s own problem-solving','The therapist must prescribe the next confrontation','The client’s account should be corrected','A causal assessment becomes urgent'],0]],audit:'PASS — Freud contrast, Weiner-Davis finding, invitation and George sequence checked against book pp. 58–60.'},
{n:25,bookTitle:'Finding out the client’s best hopes from the work',bookPage:63,steps:[
['Ask early','Within the first five minutes, the practitioner usually asks, “What are your best hopes from our talking together?” This immediately orients the meeting toward outcome.'],
['Towards, not away','“What brings you here?” commonly elicits problem talk and failed history. Best hopes asks for a future destination, like a taxi driver needing a specific destination rather than “away from the airport.”'],
['Client success criteria','The answer defines what would make therapy useful. It gives client and practitioner criteria for recognizing arrival rather than wandering without a shared endpoint.'],
['The client’s perspective is central','The contract is not derived from professional assessment. SFBT rejects a hierarchy in which the client merely states wants while the therapist determines deeper needs.'],
['Choose not to know better','With limited exceptions explored later, what the client wants is the legitimate foundation. Questions disconnected from that answer risk being impositional or impertinent.'],
['Purpose and possibility','Clients often prepare to discuss problems, not outcomes. Asking best hopes can be challenging, yet it introduces direction, purpose and possibility—conditions for brief work.']],quiz:[
['When is the best-hopes question usually asked?',['Within the first few minutes of a first meeting','Only after a complete assessment','At the final session','After the therapist sets a goal'],0],
['What does the taxi analogy demonstrate?',['A useful journey requires a destination, not merely movement away from a problem','Clients should travel to therapy','The quickest route is always obvious','Therapists should decide the destination'],0],
['How does SFBT treat the distinction between client wants and professional needs?',['It rejects the hierarchy and chooses not to know better than the client','It regards wants as superficial','It accepts needs only after diagnosis','It asks referrers to determine both'],0],
['Why does a defined outcome support brevity?',['It allows client and practitioner to notice when they have arrived','It fixes the number of sessions in advance','It prevents the client changing hopes','It eliminates all need for dialogue'],0]],audit:'PASS — timing, taxi analogy, success criteria, want/need critique and direction checked against book pp. 63–64.'},
{n:26,bookTitle:'The “contract”: a joint project',bookPage:65,steps:[
['Not a legal document','The SFBT “contract” is the collaboratively negotiated project created from the client’s answer to best hopes, not paperwork or a therapist-imposed treatment plan.'],
['Korman’s first criterion','The project must be something the client wishes to achieve. Without client ownership there is no genuinely joint direction.'],
['Korman’s second criterion','The hoped-for result must fit the practitioner’s legitimate remit. Collaboration does not erase professional role, law or service boundaries.'],
['Korman’s third criterion','It must be something client and practitioner, working well together, could reasonably hope to achieve. The project must be possible through their work.'],
['Develop broad answers','“Feel better” can become confidence, liking oneself and getting on with life. Follow-up questions turn an initial phrase into meaningful life differences.'],
['Agreement marks utility','The therapist checks whether those differences would mean the work had been useful. This confirmation makes the project explicit and shared.']],quiz:[
['Which three criteria define a workable joint project?',['Client wants it, it fits the remit, and together they could hope to achieve it','The referrer wants it, it has a diagnosis, and it takes ten sessions','It is measurable, difficult, and chosen by the therapist','It concerns symptoms, history, and homework'],0],
['What happened to the client’s initial wish to “feel better”?',['It was developed into confidence, self-liking and re-engaging with life','It was rejected as unrealistic','It became a diagnosis','It was accepted without clarification'],0],
['Why must remit be considered?',['A joint project must fit what the practitioner is legitimately able and responsible to do','The practitioner should replace the client’s hope','Remit determines the client’s values','It guarantees the desired result'],0],
['What confirms that a contract has become shared?',['The client agrees the described differences would make the work useful','The therapist writes a formulation','The referrer approves every question','The client accepts homework'],0]],audit:'PASS — all Korman criteria and chapter dialogue checked against book pp. 65–66.'},
{n:27,bookTitle:'The difference between outcome and process',bookPage:67,steps:[
['Do not stop at the route','An answer can fit remit and possibility yet still describe a therapeutic process—such as understanding or getting things off one’s chest—rather than a change in everyday life.'],
['Assume a good reason','Clients usually want a process because they expect it to lead somewhere. The practitioner is interested in the desired destination, not in prescribing or debating the assumed route.'],
['The key follow-up','“What difference will that make?” moves from offloading to feeling lighter, then energy and positivity, and finally observable activities in life.'],
['Specific routes can narrow success','“I must get a job” is one route. Asking its hoped-for difference reveals usefulness, confidence, going out and seeing people—outcomes achievable through more than one route.'],
['More routes, greater possibility','Separating destination from one tightly specified means creates manoeuvring room and increases the chances that the client recognizes success.'],
['Best hopes from, not for','The authors prefer “best hopes **from** this work.” “For” may invite what to work on—the process—whereas “from” more often invites the result.']],quiz:[
['Which answer describes process rather than an in-life outcome?',['I want to get it all off my chest','I will reconnect with friends','I will have more daily energy','I will feel useful and confident'],0],
['What question disentangles route from destination?',['What difference will that make?','When did it begin?','Who caused it?','What diagnosis explains it?'],0],
['Why does the chapter develop “I need a job” further?',['A job is one route; the desired life differences may have several routes','Employment lies outside every therapist remit','Jobs are never legitimate hopes','The client must accept volunteering instead'],0],
['Why do the authors say best hopes “from” the work?',['“From” is more likely to elicit the outcome rather than the process','It is grammatically shorter','“For” is disrespectful','It fixes the route in advance'],0]],audit:'PASS — process/outcome distinction, both dialogues and wording distinction checked against book pp. 67–69.'},
{n:28,bookTitle:'The “Great Instead”',bookPage:70,steps:[
['Negative hopes remain problem-bound','Clients naturally say they want not to feel depressed, argue, drink or think negatively. Accepting this as the contract keeps conversation tied to the unwanted state.'],
['Negation keeps the problem present','It is difficult to discuss “not depressed” without thinking about depression. Repeated problem naming may preserve a problem-saturated identity and slow change.'],
['The bridge word','The key word is **instead**: “Instead of feeling depressed, what would you like to feel or be doing?” This invites the solved state without denying the concern.'],
['Build the wanted life','A client moves from “not depressed” toward getting on with life, contacting people, going out and accepting invitations. These are positive, observable directions.'],
['Different conversations have effects','Talking about building a life may energize and enthuse; talking only about reducing depression may lower energy and invite causal rumination.'],
['A defining shift','Moving from solving or removing the problem to constructing the life that stands in its place is central—and definitional—to a solution-focused approach.']],quiz:[
['What does the “Great Instead” transform?',['A negatively framed absence of a problem into a description of wanted life','A process outcome into a diagnosis','A scale number into a task','A client hope into a therapist goal'],0],
['Why is “I won’t be depressed” a weak contract?',['Talking about not-depression still keeps depression central','Depression can never change','Negative grammar is prohibited','It is outside every practitioner’s remit'],0],
['Which question best uses “instead”?',['Instead of isolating, what would you be doing?','Why did the isolation begin?','How severe is the isolation?','Who notices your depression most?'],0],
['What shift does the chapter call definitional?',['From removing the problem to building the solved state','From individual to family sessions','From talking to written homework','From brief to time-limited therapy'],0]],audit:'PASS — negative-contract rationale, instead dialogue, energy contrast and definitional shift checked against book pp. 70–71.'},
{n:29,bookTitle:'When the client’s hope is beyond the therapist’s remit',bookPage:72,steps:[
['Start by accepting','If a client wants rehousing or never to attend school, first acknowledge that the hope makes sense or reflects genuine difficulty. Do not begin by correcting it.'],
['Clarify role honestly','The practitioner states the real boundary: no influence over housing, or a responsibility to support education. Transparency prevents false promises.'],
['Ask the hoped-for difference','Explore what the unavailable outcome would change. Rehousing might mean less arguing, lower stress, more patience, managing better and seeing light ahead.'],
['Negotiate the nearest viable hope','Ask whether beginning to experience those life differences before rehousing would make the work useful. The new project remains as close as possible to the original wish.'],
['Do not erase the original','The client may still primarily want rehousing. The therapist openly respects that while agreeing to work on light, coping and managing within the available remit.'],
['Three-step discipline','The sequence is accept the answer, clarify role and negotiate a project that fits both the client’s meaning and the practitioner’s legitimate influence.']],quiz:[
['What is the first response to a hope outside the therapist’s remit?',['Acknowledge and accept why it makes sense','Reject it as unrealistic','Replace it with a clinical target','Contact the responsible agency without discussion'],0],
['What follows acknowledgement?',['Clarify the practitioner’s role and limits','Promise the desired external result','Begin a causal assessment','End the meeting'],0],
['How was the rehousing hope made workable?',['The therapist explored its hoped-for life differences and negotiated progress toward those','The therapist applied to housing personally','The client withdrew the wish','The problem was reframed as imaginary'],0],
['What happens to the client’s original hope?',['It remains respected even while a nearer viable project is agreed','It is declared irrelevant','It becomes a therapist-owned goal','It must be abandoned before work starts'],0]],audit:'PASS — three-step sequence and complete rehousing dialogue checked against book pp. 72–73.'},
{n:30,bookTitle:'When the client has been sent',bookPage:74,steps:[
['Sent does not mean without choice','Even when attendance was another person’s idea, the client has somehow agreed to enter the room. SFBT assumes there is a good reason and seeks it.'],
['Follow the decision to come','A doctor-referred client may reveal a personal hope to manage pain better. A few questions transform the referrer’s wish into a client-owned purpose.'],
['The tougher statutory example','A mother attended because social services linked therapy to the possibility of her children returning. Getting the children back—not satisfying therapy—was her central hope.'],
['Take external requirements seriously','Although she doubted changes were needed, her solicitor said the authority meant its conditions. The therapist did not argue about who was right but explored what evidence would matter.'],
['Smallest convincing signs','Arriving at foster contact on time and not smelling of alcohol were observable signs the social worker might need to see. These became possible means toward her own end.'],
['Keep means and end distinct','Behavioural requirements are not automatically the client’s goal. Work can proceed if she is willing to try them for what matters to her—the return of her children.']],quiz:[
['What assumption guides work with someone who has been sent?',['They have a good reason for agreeing to talk, which should be discovered','They are involuntary and cannot have a project','The referrer’s goal automatically becomes theirs','Attendance proves motivation for every required change'],0],
['What personal hope emerged for the doctor-referred client?',['Managing pain better','Pleasing the doctor','Ending all medical care','Understanding childhood causes'],0],
['What was the mother’s central end in the statutory example?',['Getting her children back','Accepting the social worker’s interpretation','Discussing childhood abuse','Completing a list of treatment tasks'],0],
['Which observable signs might matter to the social worker?',['Arriving on time and not smelling of alcohol','Agreeing that every change was necessary','Attending without asking questions','Describing the cause of her anger'],0]],audit:'PASS — good-reason assumption, doctor example and statutory parent example checked against book pp. 74–76.'},
{n:31,bookTitle:'Building a contract with young people',bookPage:77,steps:[
['Most young clients are sent','Children rarely originate the idea of therapy; parents, teachers, social workers or youth justice professionals usually arrange the meeting. The practitioner recognizes this openly.'],
['Begin with whose idea','Ask whose idea attendance was and what that adult hopes will result. This respectfully locates the referral context without treating the young person as resistant.'],
['Check personal relevance','After hearing that adults want an “attitude change,” ask whether that difference would also be useful to the young person. Adult hopes become a contract only through genuine buy-in.'],
['Invite anything else','Even after agreement, ask what else would make the meeting useful to the young person. Their own priorities should not disappear behind adult concerns.'],
['When there is no buy-in','If the young person sees no problem, ask what would stop the meeting being a complete waste of time. “Getting adults off my back” can provide a client-owned direction.'],
['A graceful negotiation','The aim is not to persuade the child that adults are right. It is to find an outcome the young person can endorse, even if its meaning differs from the referrer’s.']],quiz:[
['What is the recommended opening with a young person?',['Ask whose idea the meeting was','Assume the child requested therapy','Present the adult’s treatment plan','Begin with a behavioural scale'],0],
['When can an adult’s hope become part of the contract?',['When the young person agrees it would also be useful','Whenever a teacher states it','Only after a diagnosis','When every adult agrees'],0],
['What question may help when the young person rejects the adult concern?',['What would stop this being a complete waste of your time?','Why are you so resistant?','When did your attitude begin?','Which punishment would motivate you?'],0],
['What client-owned direction may emerge?',['Getting adults off their back','Proving adults are wrong','Accepting every referral goal','Ending school attendance'],0]],audit:'PASS — complete young-person contracting dialogue and no-buy-in alternative checked against book pp. 77–78.'},
{n:32,bookTitle:'When the client says “don’t know”',bookPage:79,steps:[
['A frequent response','De Shazer paired the practitioner’s frequent “What else?” with the client’s frequent “Don’t know.” It requires flexible responses, not a conclusion that the client cannot cooperate.'],
['First, wait','Especially for young people, “don’t know” may be habitual. A quiet pause often gives enough space for an answer to begin without adding pressure.'],
['Increase tentativeness','Rephrase with “think,” “might,” “imagine” or “have a guess.” Tentative language removes the demand to know with certainty and makes responding easier.'],
['Explain persistence','Normalize the difficulty and explain why knowing what would make therapy useful matters: the practitioner wants the best chance of getting the work right for this client.'],
['Borrow another perspective','Ask who knows the person best and how that person might notice usefulness without being told. Clients sometimes answer more easily through someone else’s eyes.'],
['Trace the referrer’s hopes','Ask whose idea attendance was and what that person hoped. If necessary, include the referrer to articulate hopes for negotiation; remain committed to finding the client’s good reason.']],quiz:[
['What is the simplest first response to “don’t know”?',['Wait','Repeat more loudly','Supply an answer','End the session'],0],
['Why use words such as “might” and “imagine”?',['They make the question more tentative and reduce the demand for certainty','They guarantee a correct answer','They shift control to the referrer','They test imagination'],0],
['What is an alternative-perspective question?',['How would someone who knows you best notice this was useful?','What diagnosis would your friend give you?','Why does your friend think you resist?','Would your friend attend instead?'],0],
['When might including the referrer help?',['When their hopes need to be articulated as a basis for negotiation','Whenever a client pauses briefly','To replace the client’s perspective','To enforce attendance'],0]],audit:'PASS — waiting, tentative reformulation, persistence explanation, other-person and referrer options checked against book pp. 79–80.'},
{n:33,bookTitle:'When the client’s hopes appear to be unrealistic',bookPage:81,steps:[
['Accept before questioning likelihood','A child may want separated parents reunited. The therapist first honors the difference that would make, then notes honestly that their conversation is unlikely to cause it.'],
['Ask the next best hope','Inviting a **next best hope** may reveal another meaningful possibility: friends, happiness and feeling normal within the class.'],
['Return to the meaning if needed','If no alternative appears, explore how life would differ if the original hope occurred. The underlying life difference may be achievable by another route.'],
['Scale likelihood','A 0–10 likelihood scale can help client and therapist examine whether an outcome may happen rather than having the therapist simply pronounce it unrealistic.'],
['Best you can hope for','If a rude boss is unlikely to change, ask for the best achievable response—perhaps getting through work well enough to secure a reference and move jobs.'],
['Problems and life situations','Bereavement, chronic illness and irreversible loss are life situations, not solvable problems. The workable project concerns coping, managing and living as well as possible.']],quiz:[
['What follows acknowledgement of an unlikely hope?',['Ask for the next best hope or explore the life difference behind it','Tell the client to be realistic','Adopt the hope as a guarantee','End the work'],0],
['What can a likelihood scale contribute?',['It lets the client assess possibility collaboratively','It measures symptom severity only','It proves the therapist is correct','It sets a session limit'],0],
['When a boss is very unlikely to change, what question is suggested?',['What is the best you can hope for from coming here?','Why is your boss like this?','How can we make the boss attend?','What diagnosis fits the boss?'],0],
['How does the chapter distinguish a life situation?',['It cannot be removed, but coping and living with it can change','It is a problem the client refuses to solve','It always lies outside therapy','It requires positive reframing'],0]],audit:'PASS — reunited-parent example, likelihood scale, boss example and life-situation distinction checked against book pp. 81–83.'},
{n:34,bookTitle:'What if there is a situation of risk?',bookPage:84,steps:[
['The model is non-normative','SFBT itself contains no account of how a client ought to live. Questions are normally legitimate when connected to the client’s preferred outcome, not the practitioner’s values.'],
['Risk requires an external framework','The model itself does not assess risk. An ethical practitioner must step outside it and use professional, legal and moral standards that distinguish safe from dangerous.'],
['The violence example','A woman previously beaten by her partner hopes to become assertive. That is client-owned, within remit and possible, yet increased assertiveness could trigger greater violence.'],
['Legitimately insert safety','The therapist openly adds safety: imagine assertiveness growing in a way good for the client, relationship and safety—how would she know? This is an acknowledged imposition, not hidden neutrality.'],
['If the client agrees','Safety can then be incorporated into the preferred-future description while retaining her wish for greater assertiveness.'],
['If the client refuses','A refusal creates a genuine ethical dilemma about continuing. SFBT cannot decide it; the responsible practitioner must act through safeguarding duties and professional judgement.']],quiz:[
['How does the chapter say SFBT itself responds to risk?',['It does not provide risk evaluation; the practitioner uses an external ethical framework','It automatically prioritizes safety in every question','It treats client choice as overriding law','It supplies a standardized risk scale'],0],
['Why was assertiveness potentially dangerous in the example?',['The violent partner might escalate to restore control','Assertiveness was outside therapist remit','The client did not want it','It required a diagnosis'],0],
['How is safety introduced?',['Openly and legitimately as an additional professional concern','By pretending it came from the client','Only after therapy ends','Through a compliment'],0],
['Who must resolve the dilemma if the client rejects safety as relevant?',['The ethical practitioner using professional duties','The SFBT model itself','The client alone regardless of law','A fixed language-game rule'],0]],audit:'PASS — non-normativity, external ethics, violence dialogue and refusal dilemma checked against book pp. 84–85.'},
{n:35,bookTitle:'When the practitioner is a gatekeeper to a resource',bookPage:86,steps:[
['Multiple professional roles','Health, drug, social-care and other practitioners may combine therapeutic conversation with advice, risk monitoring, assessment and decisions affecting scarce resources.'],
['A requested resource is often a route','Parents may request accommodation, classroom support or medication because they expect relief. Gatekeepers must assess whether the hoped-for changes could occur without that resource.'],
['Open beyond the proposed solution','“What difference are you hoping that will make?” translates a resource request into life outcomes and reveals alternative routes.'],
['The accommodation example','A mother wanted her son returned to care. Further questions revealed desired outcomes: reasonable return times, school attendance, quiet and happiness, and the son taking notice.'],
['Acknowledge and clarify authority','The social worker recognized severe strain and explained that no immediate decision could be made without meeting the young person and likely the family.'],
['Assessment remains real','Accommodation may still be the eventual result. But defining wished-for change makes multiple routes visible and allows a resource-free outcome to be genuinely considered.']],quiz:[
['Why ask what difference a requested resource would make?',['To reveal the underlying outcomes and possible alternative routes','To refuse the resource indirectly','To prove the client’s request is irrational','To avoid the assessment role'],0],
['Which outcomes did the mother want besides accommodation?',['School attendance, reasonable return times, quiet, happiness and being heard','A diagnosis and medication','A shorter assessment','Family members excluded from meetings'],0],
['Does the chapter rule out allocating the requested resource?',['No; it may remain necessary after alternatives are explored','Yes, resource-free outcomes are mandatory','Only medication is ruled out','Yes, requests are never client goals'],0],
['What dual responsibility does a gatekeeper hold?',['Explore client hopes while fulfilling a real assessment/allocation function','Act only as therapist','Approve every client request','Hide decision-making authority'],0]],audit:'PASS — multi-role examples, alternative-outcome question and full accommodation case checked against book pp. 86–88.'},
{n:36,bookTitle:'What if we fail to develop a joint project?',bookPage:89,steps:[
['No project, not SFBT','Without a client-specified direction, work may be effective, empowering or strengths-based, but it is not solution focused. Techniques alone do not define the approach.'],
['Why direction is necessary','The practitioner cannot know which questions, details or achievements matter without best hopes. In a non-normative model, therapist-selected direction has no internal claim to be “right.”'],
['Legitimate worker-directed work','Statutory agencies may begin with concerns the client does not share. The worker must state those concerns and consequences openly rather than disguising agency goals as client goals.'],
['Seeking buy-in','A worker can link minimum requirements to something the client values—for example the wish to remain out of hospital—and ask whether they will work together on the needed changes.'],
['Joint mini-projects','Listen for complaints that imply a wanted difference. “Getting out more” may become a genuine small client project within a larger agency-directed intervention.'],
['Twin-track work','The agency track remains explicit while a client track asks what could make unavoidable meetings worthwhile. Twin-tracking offers more cooperation than agency direction alone.']],quiz:[
['Can using SFBT techniques alone make work solution focused?',['No, a client-defined joint project is essential','Yes, if a scale is included','Yes, when the worker is strengths-based','Only in statutory work'],0],
['How should statutory concerns be handled?',['State them and their consequences openly while seeking client buy-in','Hide them inside questions','Abandon them to remain non-normative','Treat them as the client’s stated hopes'],0],
['What is a joint mini-project?',['A client-owned wanted difference within broader worker-directed work','A shortened statutory assessment','A task chosen by the agency','A meeting without a goal'],0],
['What does twin-tracking preserve?',['Both the explicit agency mandate and something worthwhile from the client’s viewpoint','Two therapists in every meeting','Two competing diagnoses','Separate progress scales only'],0]],audit:'PASS — definitional boundary, statutory buy-in, mini-projects and twin-tracking checked against book pp. 89–90.'},
{n:37,bookTitle:'Preferred futures: the “Tomorrow Question”',bookPage:93,steps:[
['From outcome to lived description','After best hopes establish purpose, the practitioner elicits everyday life if those hopes were realized. Detailed future talk became valued for its therapeutic effect, not just as a stopping criterion.'],
['A virtual experience','Clear description can operate like an experience of possibility. Because it spans daily life rather than one narrow goal, the authors prefer **preferred future** to “goals.”'],
['Not literally the solution','The preferred future is an alternative way of living in which the presenting issue has little part. The actual solution emerges through life and may not match the description exactly.'],
['Mother and daughter','A wish that a daughter come home on time becomes a better relationship. Describing that relationship may improve negotiation without the therapist directly solving curfew rules.'],
['From Miracle to Tomorrow','Once best hopes already introduce hope, a miracle metaphor becomes less necessary. Ask: if you woke tomorrow with the relationship hoped for, what would be the first sign?'],
['Keep the client centre stage','The Tomorrow Question is less dramatic and less memorable than miracle or magic-wand language, allowing the client’s words—and agency—to remain prominent.']],quiz:[
['Why did the authors adopt “preferred future” rather than “goal”?',['The description covers a broad alternative way of living, not only a narrow target','Goals are never observable','Preferred futures predict exact behaviour','The term guarantees hope'],0],
['Is a preferred-future description itself the solution?',['No, it is an alternative life description from which solutions may emerge','Yes, every described action must be performed','Only when a miracle metaphor is used','Yes, it replaces all real-life negotiation'],0],
['How was the curfew complaint developed?',['Into a description of a better mother–daughter relationship','Into a stricter rule prescribed by the therapist','Into a diagnosis of the daughter','Into a ten-session family contract'],0],
['Why does BRIEF often use the Tomorrow Question?',['Best hopes already create hope, and simpler wording keeps attention on the client','Miracles are considered unethical','It is used only for distant futures','It avoids detailed descriptions'],0]],audit:'PASS — preferred-future rationale, mother/daughter example and Miracle-to-Tomorrow transition checked against book pp. 93–94.'},
{n:38,bookTitle:'Distant futures',bookPage:95,steps:[
['Some hopes cannot arrive tomorrow','Becoming wealthy, finding a partner, obtaining a job, passing a distant exam or having a child returned from care cannot necessarily be complete the next morning.'],
['Ask for movement in the direction','Modify the Tomorrow Question: imagine life has begun moving toward the distant hope. This preserves the client’s aspiration while asking for an immediate credible beginning.'],
['The teenage boy’s hope','A boy in residential care wanted to wake as a millionaire with a Porsche and beautiful girlfriend. The therapist invited a smaller miracle that started movement in that direction.'],
['Behaviour follows possibility','The boy dismissed the conversation and left, yet next day rose before midday, bought a newspaper and looked for work for the first time in weeks.'],
['A practical rule of thumb','Confidence, happiness, better relationships, school effort and good parenting can begin tomorrow because they can show in current life.'],
['Flexible, not rigid','The distinction is a rule of thumb, not law. The therapist judges whether to ask for the hope achieved or for first signs of movement toward it.']],quiz:[
['How should a Tomorrow Question address a distant hope?',['Ask what would show life beginning to move in that direction','Declare it unrealistic','Describe it as fully achieved tomorrow','Replace it with the therapist’s goal'],0],
['What did the residential-care teenager do the next day?',['Got up, bought a paper and looked for work','Purchased a Porsche','Returned for another session','Completed a progress scale'],0],
['Which hope could plausibly begin tomorrow according to the rule of thumb?',['Acting with greater confidence','Already passing an exam scheduled next year','Having a child legally returned overnight','Already becoming a millionaire'],0],
['How firm is this distinction?',['It is a flexible rule of thumb','It is an absolute clinical rule','It applies only to adolescents','It depends on diagnosis'],0]],audit:'PASS — distant-future examples, residential-care case and rule of thumb checked against book p. 95.'},
{n:39,bookTitle:'The qualities of well-described preferred futures: the client’s perspective',bookPage:96,steps:[
['Five qualities, first three here','A full preferred future is positive, concrete/observable, detailed, multi-perspectival and interactional. This chapter develops the first three from the client’s own perspective.'],
['Positive means present','Positive is mathematical, not cheerful: describe something present rather than an absence. “Not running” does not reveal whether the child walks or talks with friends.'],
['Do not assume the replacement','A schoolboy said talking to friends would replace running and walking over to them would replace shouting. The wanted alternative must come from the client.'],
['Translate feelings into action','Broad inner states such as confidence gain therapeutic impact when translated into visible behaviour. The same word means different actions for different clients.'],
['Three distinct confidence signs','Susan would open curtains and answer the door; Nina would visit the library; James would notice breakfast-cereal taste. Close questioning makes each future personal and observable.'],
['Add time, place and context','Detail makes possibility realistic. Ask where and when a respectful encounter occurs, perhaps 8:15 in the kitchen and a daughter saying good morning.']],quiz:[
['What does “positive” mean in this chapter?',['Something present and wanted rather than the absence of a problem','Optimistic thinking','Praising every client answer','Ignoring negative emotion'],0],
['Why should the therapist not assume walking replaces running?',['The client may name a different replacement, such as talking with friends','Walking is never observable','The therapist must avoid school examples','Running has no interactional meaning'],0],
['Which was Susan’s concrete sign of confidence?',['Opening her curtains and answering the door','Going to the library','Tasting breakfast cereal','Saying good morning at 8:15'],0],
['What makes a future description detailed?',['Time, place, action and context','A diagnostic label','A broad feeling word alone','The therapist’s interpretation'],0]],audit:'PASS — five-quality frame, replacement examples, three confidence examples and kitchen detail checked against book pp. 96–97.'},
{n:40,bookTitle:'The qualities of well-described preferred futures: other person perspectives',bookPage:98,steps:[
['Two relational qualities','This chapter adds **multi-perspectival** and **interactional** detail to the positive, observable and detailed client-perspective description.'],
['What others would witness','Ask how family, friends, colleagues, neighbours or passers-by would know change occurred. Their observations give outward evidence of inner states.'],
['A passer-by’s view','A severely depressed person imagines strangers seeing a man with his head up, catching their eye and perhaps smiling—small actions associated with a life worth living.'],
['Responses and effects','Interactional description goes beyond observation: how would another respond, how would the client respond back, and what difference would each response make?'],
['The morning exchange','A daughter says good morning; the mother answers and smiles; the daughter smiles back; relief may lead to hugs and tears. The preferred future becomes a reciprocal sequence.'],
['Possibility, not micro-management','The purpose is not to prescribe these exact behaviours as tasks. Successful life may look similar but different; detailed talk creates possibility rather than a script.']],quiz:[
['What makes a description multi-perspectival?',['It includes what other people would observe','It contains several diagnoses','It lists several therapist interpretations','It uses more than one scale'],0],
['What might passers-by notice in the example?',['Head up, eye contact and perhaps a smile','A completed homework sheet','The absence of all emotion','A conversation with the therapist'],0],
['What additional feature makes a description interactional?',['It traces reciprocal responses and their effects','It names an individual feeling','It states a time and place only','It predicts exact compliance'],0],
['Are the morning behaviours prescribed tasks?',['No, they create a realistic sense of possibility','Yes, mother and daughter must enact them exactly','Only the smile is mandatory','They are assessment criteria'],0]],audit:'PASS — multi-perspectival evidence, passer-by example, reciprocal morning sequence and non-prescriptive purpose checked against book pp. 98–99.'}
];
